API Gateway vs Load Balancer vs Reverse Proxy 🔄

These three are commonly confused in backend/system design interviews. Let’s clarify their differences.

1️⃣ API Gateway 🚪

Purpose: Single entry point for clients in a microservices architecture, handling routing and cross-cutting concerns.

Responsibilities:

Request routing to specific services

Authentication & Authorization (JWT / OAuth2)

Rate limiting / throttling

Load balancing across service instances

Request/response transformation

Logging & monitoring

Optional caching

Example:

Client → API Gateway → User / Order / Payment Service

Used in: Microservices, mobile apps, web apps.
Tools: Spring Cloud Gateway, Kong, Zuul

2️⃣ Load Balancer ⚖️

Purpose: Distributes traffic evenly across multiple instances of the same service for availability and performance.

Responsibilities:

Balances incoming requests

Health check of service instances

Simple routing (usually not content-aware)

Example:

Client → Load Balancer → User Service Instance 1
                           → User Service Instance 2

Used in: Any system with multiple instances of a service (monoliths or microservices).
Tools: NGINX, AWS ELB, HAProxy.

Key Difference: A load balancer doesn’t handle authentication, rate limiting, or API transformations—it just distributes traffic.

3️⃣ Reverse Proxy 🔄

Purpose: Acts as an intermediary between clients and servers, often for security, caching, or SSL termination.

Responsibilities:

Forward requests from clients to backend servers

SSL termination (HTTPS offloading)

Caching and compression

Can hide internal server details

Optional simple load balancing

Example:

Client → Reverse Proxy → Web Server / API Server

Used in: Security, caching, and performance optimization.
Tools: NGINX, Apache HTTP Server, Traefik.

🔹 Comparison Table
Feature	API Gateway	Load Balancer	Reverse Proxy
Routing to services	✅	Limited	✅
Auth & Authorization	✅	❌	❌
Rate Limiting	✅	❌	❌
Load Balancing	✅	✅	Optional
Request/Response Transformation	✅	❌	Limited
Logging & Monitoring	✅	Limited	Limited
Caching	Optional	❌	✅
Use Case	Microservices entry point	Scale service instances	Security, caching, SSL
